{"id":"dal-earnings-q3-2026","title":"DAL earnings — Delta Air Lines, Q3 2026","section":"Earnings","date":"2026-10-09","html":"<p>Delta says demand is the strongest part of its story and fuel is the whole problem: it cut its full-year profit range by more than a dollar a share, and every dollar of that is jet fuel.</p>\n<p>Reported Oct 9, 2026. Next report: date not found (January). Updated Nov 11, 2026. Research, not investment advice.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-short-version\">The short version</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Demand:</strong> stronger. More than 60% of Q4 was already booked; forward cash sales up nearly 20%.</li><li><strong>Guidance:</strong> cut. Full-year EPS $5.10–5.60, was $6.50–7.50.</li><li><strong>Biggest worry:</strong> fuel. About $6B of extra fuel cost this year.</li><li><strong>Chart:</strong> leans up while it holds $87, with a ceiling at $92.50 and the 52-week high at $95.18.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-numbers\">The numbers</h3>\n<ul><li>Adjusted revenue $17.6B, up 16%. Adjusted EPS $1.72, against $1.70 a year ago.</li><li>GAAP revenue $20.2B and EPS $1.15.</li><li>No beat or miss is called: Webull's estimates ($1.78, $18.92B) mix GAAP and adjusted bases.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-said-about-the-road-ahead\">What management said about the road ahead</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Q4:</strong> revenue up about 20%, operating margin 7% to 9%, EPS $1.15 to $1.65.</li><li><strong>Bookings:</strong> early Q1 2027 bookings look similar to Q4.</li><li><strong>Pricing:</strong> unit revenue up 15.4%. Premium revenue up about 18%, and main cabin unit revenue up in the high teens, its third straight quarter of improvement.</li><li><strong>Corporate travel:</strong> up at a double-digit rate in every sector.</li><li><strong>Capacity:</strong> held back on purpose. Q4 seats grow less than 2%, with fewer main cabin seats.</li><li><strong>Costs other than fuel:</strong> up 7.3% per seat mile; management expects low single-digit growth in 2027.</li><li>In the CEO's words: &quot;Our resilience reflects the structural durability we've built over many years&quot; (Ed Bastian, press release).</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-is-worried-about\">What management is worried about</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Fuel.</strong> Q3 fuel averaged $3.61 a gallon, up about 60%, and ran more than $500M above what Delta itself guided in July. Q4 is guided at about $4.25 a gallon, and that is after a benefit of about $0.40 a gallon from the refinery Delta owns.</li><li><strong>Competitor capacity.</strong> Named as a risk. United expects industry capacity to accelerate in 2027.</li><li><strong>Air traffic control delays and weather.</strong></li><li><strong>The economy.</strong></li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"the-big-worry-fuel-both-ways\">The big worry: fuel, both ways</h3>\n<p><strong>What would push fuel lower</strong></p>\n<ul><li><strong>The Strait of Hormuz reopening.</strong> Exxon's CEO expects it to reopen but gave no date.</li><li><strong>Refineries coming back.</strong> Chevron says about 10% of world refining is offline and expects margins to stay high near term, then ease. Jet fuel is a refined product, so this matters more to Delta than the crude price does.</li><li><strong>Crude already off its high.</strong> Brent topped $110 in late October and was about $92.50 on Nov 11. The oil fund USO closed at $141.47 on Nov 11, down 16% from $168.83 on Oct 30.</li><li><strong>Producers are not betting on it lasting.</strong> Occidental plans on $50 oil. None of Exxon, Chevron or Occidental raised spending.</li><li><strong>A Fed that keeps hiking</strong> cools demand for everything, fuel included.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>What would push fuel higher</strong></p>\n<ul><li><strong>Hormuz staying restricted.</strong> Fed Governor Waller says insurance and cargo transfers there add $20 to $30 a barrel. A tanker hit a mine in the Strait on Nov 11.</li><li><strong>The product shortage lasting.</strong> Exxon's CEO said normalising runs well through next year, with roughly half of Russia's refining offline.</li><li><strong>No new supply.</strong> With no producer raising spending, there is no quick supply response.</li><li><strong>A low strategic reserve,</strong> which the Fed's own material flags.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>How Delta is placed against it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Its refinery is worth about $0.40 a gallon in Q4. United has no such offset and recovered only about 72% of its fuel increase through fares.</li><li>Delta's CEO says the industry is recovering fuel faster than in past cycles.</li><li>The pattern to watch: Delta's fuel bill follows refined-product prices, not just crude. Crude falling while refineries stay tight would help less than the headline suggests.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"money-plans\">Money plans</h3>\n<ul><li>Capital spending $1,408M in Q3. Full-year figure: not found.</li><li>More than $2B of debt paid down in 2026. Adjusted net debt $13.35B.</li><li>Dividend continued ($141M in Q3). No buybacks mentioned.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"people-and-governance\">People and governance</h3>\n<p>Not yet researched for Delta. From this page on, every earnings page will carry this section: who runs the company and for how long, whether a successor is named, and how much rests on one person.</p>\n<h3 id=\"does-the-chart-agree\">Does the chart agree?</h3>\n<figure class=\"chart\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" data-ticker=\"DAL\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 400\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"DAL · daily · the fuel-versus-demand range chart\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\"><text x=\"8\" y=\"18\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#20242C\" class=\"c-ink\">DAL · daily · the fuel-versus-demand range</text><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"374.0\" y2=\"374.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"289.0\" y2=\"289.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"204.0\" y2=\"204.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-gr